213:"I should drink from the upper one," he said, when they came up. "Something has used the other. It was heavy, and it had a foot as broad as that, with no claw to it, and it came down off the slope to do it and went back the same way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:385:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:27:The foot belonged to a man who drove the oxen, and one of them had stood on it. The boy had gone off a hay-rick onto his shoulder. She set that bone with her thumbs before she mended it, and told him first that he would mind it, and he bit his scarf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:83:"They're the same thing, done twice as hard. That's the short one, and a healing house calls it the surface reading. Hands on the hurt, or on a wrist, and a count of ten. It tells me what's broken and where it's standing, and it costs me nothing I'd notice; I did eight of them along that bench." Oryn leaned on the edge of the ash. "There's a long one, which they call the deep reading. Both hands, and all of me, and it isn't a count, it's minutes. I go through the whole of how a person's built, as you'd go through every room of a house with a lamp. It costs what a mending costs. And the one under my hands has to lie still for it, and be whole, since a break shouts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:185:The route was the only thing she had ever made. She thought of that on the road, where there was time. She had not been given it. There had been no round when she crossed the Line, only a rumour in a ferry-town that the holds had no hands; and she had gone to the first gate, and mended what was there, and asked where the next fence was. It had been four holds, the first year. She had put the fifth on in her third round, and the sixth for an old woman's sake. The seventh she had added because of a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:327:*Mine: three ribs taped, twenty-one days from the seventy-eighth; the calf bound in her cloth. Lira: a leg that is new, an ankle that is taped and will not be mended. Brom: mended, and not to pull. Karis: empty, and filling. Seln: nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:329:*The nine: held, on a mountain and off it. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:15:Brom leaned on his rope through all of it, and Cael thought that he had never seen him so content. He had stood idle in a yard for five days and in a meadow for eleven. Here he had found a way of putting his whole weight to use that a woman with a needle could not have forbidden, and he had found it by himself. Iron Skin had not once risen to meet a load on that shelf. Cael had his own things to keep down there, for Compression rose whenever the rope snatched, and Wind leaned at every yard that Lira ran, and he paid the rent on both fragments and spent neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:27:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:*The nine: held, the whole length of it. Anchor asked at the seventh as it has never asked. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:43:"The hold stands," he said. "No Path and no fragment, for any of us, here as on a road; that's not new. What's new is that until somebody can tell me which kind of loud, we keep every kind of quiet as well. Low voices. No iron on iron. And nobody goes under anything with a back until we've looked at it first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:133:He walked. Nine fragments had come up at the snatch, and he went on down the slope with the rope in his hands, putting them down one at a time. Compression had felt the jerk come along the hemp and had offered to take it through his frame into the hill. Anchor had been asking about the island since first light. *There?* A point fixed against that notch, and told to stay, would have been a better thing than any log; it would not have walked, or worn, or needed a man's weight to hold it. He owned the craft of it. He told it that the log was Brom's, and to leave it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:*The nine: held, on a hill where three of them would have been of use. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:She had gone up to the eave on purpose to be first. Cael came after her at his own pace, which on level grass was anybody's pace now, and he took his inventory on the way, as he took it at the start of anything. The calf was a leg again on the flat; he had asked it the evening before, on the saddle road, and it had told him nine strides uphill and then shut. Brom's forearm was closed along the whole of its seam and wrapped by Brom's own choice, and it was to pull nothing. Lira had wound a strip of linen round her right ankle, over the stocking, and she walked on the whole sole of that foot. Karis had two pink lines across each palm and held a pencil for a quarter of an hour at a time. The nine fragments lay in him as they had lain since the ford, idle, each with its rent paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:69:It was work, besides, that four half-mended people could do. A man with a bitten calf could pace a level shelf and write. A girl with a stiff ankle could tell him whether a hundred yards of it would bear her, and a researcher who could hold a pencil for a quarter of an hour had a cord, and a clock, and a head that kept numbers as a jar keeps water. Nobody would have to be fast, or hard, or to set anything alight; the job asked for no Path from any of them, and for no fragment of his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:203:*The nine: held. No Path has been lifted by any of us since the ford. Deployment since the Line: one.*
