editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:15:Brom leaned on his rope through all of it, and Cael thought that he had never seen him so content. He had stood idle in a yard for five days and in a meadow for eleven. Here he had found a way of putting his whole weight to use that a woman with a needle could not have forbidden, and he had found it by himself. Iron Skin had not once risen to meet a load on that shelf. Cael had his own things to keep down there, for Compression rose whenever the rope snatched, and Wind leaned at every yard that Lira ran, and he paid the rent on both fragments and spent neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:71:He looked down into it. The sides went down smooth and close, without a ledge, and where they passed out of the light there was no floor that he could see. Karis let a pebble go, and it clicked from one wall to the other for a count of three and was still clicking when he lost it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:123:"I've stopped arguing," said Karis. "I'm only counting now." She had his left arm turned to the light already. The cloth was dry at the wrist, and she let it go. "Eight. Nine feet. Broken across, clean, two feet wide, no bottom found. Cart: taken over on one man's back, with four to steady her." She stopped. "And then I shall write that a carter by himself does not cross it, and that two planks of oak might serve, eight feet long, and that I haven't tried them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:169:They went back to the fire. Brom had the bacon out, and Karis was holding the long leaf to the flames to read down her last column, and up on the slope the scraping had begun to slow as the cold came on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:Lira had gone on ahead. She had not eaten. She had wound the linen round her right ankle by the first light there was, and tried her weight on it, and walked away along the foot of the wall with her arms swinging loose. By the time the cart was a hundred yards out she was a small dark figure at the place where the bay shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:27:She was. She went out along the ledge under its roof of rock, close in, and from that distance there was nothing to show that one foot came down later than the other; she looked like a thing that the mountain had been keeping that shelf for. He made himself look at the fin. It stood ahead of them and a little to the right, pale in the half light, with the frozen shale banked along its upper side, and he judged that they were thirty yards short of its nearer end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:It was Wind, and it was hers, and it was the whole of it. He had not heard that sound from her since a painted floor at Norhold, and she had never, there or anywhere, let it go so hard. She was not on the falling stone. She was twenty feet east of it, in the air, and then she was standing on sound ledge with both arms flung wide. Behind her a hundred feet of shelf was going down into the blue with a roar that grew as it fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:Wind stood up in his own feet to answer hers. He put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:He gave it no more than a breath's looking, and that was enough. The front had come a third of the way, and it was no longer grey; it had a brown smoke of dust standing over it, and out of the smoke came the sound, a deep steady grinding with the hail of the plates on top. He passed Brom going the other way, and Brom's eyes went to him and to the cart and he did not waste his wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:All nine of them were up. He had never felt them so, on the stretch or at the ford; there the offers had come one behind another, and here they came in a single shout. Wind wanted his feet and had the whole distance measured. Storm showed him the air over the bay, torn already, as a lane. He put them down as he ran, without words, since there was no time for words, and they got up again at every stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:181:The stream had spread as it reached the level. He saw the backs of perhaps a dozen animals, wheeling on the bay's floor in a fog of dust, with the shale still running between their legs like water; and beyond them, alone, in the place where five people had stood a minute ago, the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:So she bound the ankle in the dark, on the seventy-eighth morning, and ate nothing, as she had eaten nothing before a card. The foot was what it was. It bent less than the other and later, and she had stopped being angry with it somewhere on the bench and begun simply to allow for it, as one allows for a wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:The rock was a gift. It was dry under its roof, and rimed along the outer half where the roof stopped, so she kept in, close under the wall, as she had promised a fire that she would. Her left foot struck and her right came after it. She had the count in her legs. Forty. Fifty. The gorge went by on her right hand, blue and enormous, and she gave it none of her attention; she was reading the shelf five yards ahead of her toes, for cracks and for colour, as she had read a sprung floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:259:She had been told at Fenmark that a burst was a choice, and at Norhold she had made a rank of choosing well. For sixty days on gravel bars and roads she had refused it a stride at a time, on purpose, and she had been proud of how good she had got. Not one of those refusals was anywhere near her now. The ground went, and her body did what a body with Wind in it does when the ground goes, and it did not send up to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:287:Wind said *now*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:313:*Now*, said Wind, for the last time, and it was not reasonable any longer. It was begging.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:Something the size of a cart went behind her so close that the wind of it turned her half round. She kept her legs. She was three strides from the stone, with her arm already out for it, when the shale that the thing had brought down with it took her ankles from under her like a wave on a beach, and she went onto her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:It was grey in the hollow still. He had got to his feet by the wall, as he did every morning, to learn what the night had done to him, and he had drawn in air to say something to Brom. At a certain depth, a little short of where the ribs had stopped him the day before, a hand seemed to close across his left side and hold it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:9:"That's all it does," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:25:"He'll go to draw a full breath, or turn to pick something up, and that pulls before the bone does, so he stops there. He'll have learned it by tonight, and he'll hate it by noon tomorrow, and it stays on till I take it off." She looked at him at last. "Twenty-one days from the morning they went, and I want to be the one who lifts it. Can you count that far?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:"Because I cannot mend what I can't read." The healer said it without heat. "I read her leg at a count of ten and I knew what it was, and how she was built round it, and where the thing that knits had stopped; and then I knew where to put mine. I had my hands on him for three times as long and I got bone. I'd have got that much with my eyes shut, off a dead sheep." She showed her palms, briefly. "Past the bone there's nothing I can lay hold of. I'd be pushing in the dark, and I don't do that to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:35:She turned to Brom then, and had his left arm out of its sleeve before he could offer it. The seam was dry and pale. She wound a strip of plain linen round the wrist, four turns, no tighter than a cuff, and tied it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:43:Cael let his shirt down over five bands of linen. It was a strange thing to have somebody else's judgment fastened round his chest. He had always priced his own hurts, alone, and decided what each would bear, and he had been wrong often enough to carry the marks. This one had been priced for him by a person who could not read him, and she had put the price where he would feel it at every breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:93:There was something else that wanted to help him, and it was harder to turn away. The Iron read would have given him every plate on that lip within a pace and a half: which was seated on rock and which on its fellow, and which meant to go. He would have known the ground as he knew his own weight. He had refused it on a fan, when all that hung on it was a map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:He turned round, since the others had. From the western ledge the whole bay lay open behind them. He could see the pale rock standing across its floor, and the two black lines above it that came to a point, and the wide scoured strip of the hill; and along the foot of the face, in a row, with a length and a half between each, the shale-backs were mowing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:They slept that night beside a seep of good water, halfway down, in a fold of the hill where somebody had once built a wall of loose stones against the wind. Seln had found it, and he had a fire lit when they came up, and enough dead heather gathered to keep it till morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:157:The track had come down by then onto the last long slant above the valley, and Lira's right foot hung from the pack-saddle at about the height of the healer's shoulder, bare, and wound with the strip of linen that it had worn since a meadow. Oryn went beside it for the better part of a mile. Cael, two paces behind, saw her eyes go to it and come away, and go again, as a person's go to a thing that somebody else has done badly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:207:"That's all tape ever does," said Oryn. "Take it off. It's your turn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:319:He could not read. Cael saw that in the first breath, from where the brown eyes went; they passed over the six columns to the two ruled lines down the middle of the leaf, and to what was drawn between them, and a thick cracked finger came down on the foot of a fan and began to climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:329:He went on. They told him each mark as his finger reached it, in a word or two, as one names the stops on a road; and Karis, at his elbow, read him the number of each narrowing from her separate column. He stopped a long while at the seventh. He stopped longer at the eighth, where Cael had drawn a short black bar across both lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:19:"It's not come through," said Brom. He had looked at a good many hurts on a good many yards, and he said it as the one thing in sight that was better than it might have been. "I'll not pull it straight, for I don't know how, and I'd do it badly. But it wants keeping as it is, till there's somebody who does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:He chose the splints himself, and they were the mountain's own; he found two plates in the bank above the wall, each as long as her shin and no thicker than a slate, and he laid one down either side of the leg. They had no canvas to tear, for the canvas had gone with the cart. Cael's coat had lost its left sleeve at a river. He got the knife into the seam of the right one, and Seln cut that as well, and Brom bound the two plates on with it in three places, one-handed, with Seln's fingers for the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:55:It was a cold night, and they had no blankets; the frost came down out of a clear sky, and they lay close under the rock in a row, the hurt ones in the middle, and kept one another warm as cattle do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:71:"Does it hurt?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:93:He read it through, and he was not ashamed of it, for it had been true when the pencil made it; but he could find no sentence to set beneath it that was a thing counted, and he would not put down one that was only a thing felt. He shut the book, and that day got no line either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:101:"That's today, and tomorrow, and the day after. Five a row." He pointed with the knife. "The bacon's for whoever's walking. I've done it in my head six ways and that's the one that comes out even."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:119:"Does it go?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:Brom poured for her out of the skin. She washed to the wrists, rubbing hard, and it was while she did so that Cael read her; she had read him first, which did not often happen to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:223:There was nothing to see. He had half expected a light, or a colour, as there was grey in Iron Skin and yellow in a point; and there was a square brown woman kneeling in the dust with her hands on a girl's leg, breathing slowly. A minute went by, and another, and the only thing that changed was that the sweat came out on her forehead, though the air was cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:231:"What she mends with. Everybody's got it. It goes round you like water round a mill, and when you break a bone it does the knitting, if you leave it be for six weeks." Oryn spoke in pieces, between breaths, with her eyes shut. "At a break it stands, since it can't get by, and it piles up, and that's the heat you felt in this last night. I find where it's standing. Then I open the way with mine, and hers goes through, and I hold it open while it does the six weeks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:269:It was her own bread, out of the mule's off pannier, a dark close loaf from some hold's oven, and she broke it in six and gave five pieces away before she bit into hers. There was a cheese as well, and four onions. Brom looked at the three rows on the lid of the box, and then at the pannier, and swept the rows back into his bag without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:283:She went to Karis last of the three, on her knees, and read her as she had read the leg, with two fingers on the inside of a thin wrist and two on the side of the neck. Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:311:Seln inclined his head. He looked, Cael thought, like a man who has had a report read by somebody competent, and it had been a long time since that had happened to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:343:He watched her face, and he knew what it was doing, for he had seen a brass plate in a district hall wait eleven seconds over a boy of fourteen before it lit. He had seen assessors look at a panel with that same small stillness, and a hooded seat, and a clerk with a form that had no box for him. It was what an instrument looked like when it reached for the thing it was made to read, and closed on air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:351:"Whatever you are, I can't read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:383:*The healer put her hands on my ribs and could not read me. She told me so to my face, and asked nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:"I asked him that in those words. He said, the kind a man would not be on a road." Seln spread his hands a little. "He means all three, and he does not know which. It is the rule that Thornwater keeps, for the reason that Thornwater gave us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:41:Cael looked at the slope for a long count. Nothing on it moved. A small wind came down the gorge and stirred the heather, and across the water the far wall gave back the sound of the stream, late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:73:"Floats. A wheel's for a road. It wants something hard under it to turn on. There's nothing hard in that." He pushed his boot into the fan and watched the plates close round it. "She'll not roll on it. She sits in it, like a boat in a pond, and she goes where it goes, and it only knows the one way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:77:"I never said that. You don't row a boat up a weir. You put a line on her bow from the top of the weir, and you lift her head, and you let her swim up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:127:In his own feet Wind leaned with hers, as it always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:135:The Iron read was harder to refuse, because it offered the thing he was being paid to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:173:Karis measured where he stopped. She had her cord for the width, knotted at every foot. For the pitch she had made a thing out of the brass straightedge and a pebble hung on a thread, and she laid the brass across the slope and read off how far the thread hung from its mark. It took her the better part of a quarter of an hour, and she spoke each number once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:199:"I am of no use at a shaft that she is not of more," he had said, with a small bow toward Lira. "I cannot run, or carry, or count by a clock. There is one part of a day's work on a road that I can do better than any of you, which is to go on in front and learn where a person would be glad to lie down. I should like to do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:243:He saw her stop, sixty yards off. She stood quite still for as long as it would have taken to count ten. Then she set no stone on its end; she turned, and walked back toward them along the inside of the shelf, slowly, close under the wall, and her feet made no sound on the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:249:A quarter of a mile along, and far up the slope, another shelf ran across the face. It was narrower than theirs. Along it, for the length of a long field, the grey plates of the hillside lay in a row, humped and overlapping, each heap about the size of the cart; and there was a gap of bare rock between each heap and the next, and all the gaps were the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:3:The paper was on the oak before the mist had left the river, and Lira read it with one boot on and the other in her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:She had gone up to the eave on purpose to be first. Cael came after her at his own pace, which on level grass was anybody's pace now, and he took his inventory on the way, as he took it at the start of anything. The calf was a leg again on the flat; he had asked it the evening before, on the saddle road, and it had told him nine strides uphill and then shut. Brom's forearm was closed along the whole of its seam and wrapped by Brom's own choice, and it was to pull nothing. Lira had wound a strip of linen round her right ankle, over the stocking, and she walked on the whole sole of that foot. Karis had two pink lines across each palm and held a pencil for a quarter of an hour at a time. The nine fragments lay in him as they had lain since the ford, idle, each with its rent paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:15:"I told you to read it first," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:17:He had come out with the book under his arm and his coat on, and he set the flat stone on the page before he so much as glanced at the new slip. He had read it by lamplight when the carter brought it, and had no need to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:23:Pike read it out for the meadow, and then he read it again from the first word, with his shoulder turned to the tents. There were not many to hear. The dry roads had emptied the grass to seven fires, and at most of those people were still asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:43:"What does he call a map?" she said. "I've a registry sheet in the middle box with the whole continent on it, printed, and a calfskin that a carter drew for his own wheels. They aren't the same kind of thing. Which does he want?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:49:Cael read the slip through once more, for the thing that was not in it. There was no name on it but the hold's. Nobody had asked to have it kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:There were fourteen slips. He gave each the same attention, the bark and the hide and the two shingles, and he sorted them as he read, as he had once sorted a board in Ardenmere by what each card would cost him. There was a fence to be set for a steading on the north water, at five marks, and a wheel to be fetched for a carter on the south road, and hay to be got in at the upper inn for a mark and the loft to sleep in. A string of mules wanted walking westward to the turf fire, and that slip he passed over without stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:61:The second was that nobody's finger was on it. The last large slip they took had carried a captain's name in capitals. A one-armed man had struck that name with his own pen before a meadow, and they had gone down to a river under the weight of it. This one had been posted for anyone at all, with no hold on it. If they took it they would take it off nobody, and the captain at the top of the sheet could read it when he came down and find nothing in it that had been his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:73:"Does it need the cart?" he said. "We've none. We could walk your bench and measure it. She has a cord, and I can pace a yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:81:"A cord'll tell him how wide it is. It'll not tell him what a wheel does on it. The paper says *with a cart*, and he'll ask you at his gate where yours is, and I'd have the answer ready."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:161:The cart went up to the saddle between two people. It had no beast, for there was not a mule in Lowmarch to be hired for a mountain by a crew that might not bring it back. Seln took the near shaft and Cael the off, and they leaned, and the narrow wheels went round. On the flat of the meadow it was nothing. On the hill Cael counted nine strides and felt the calf begin to close, and at the tenth Lira's shoulder came in beside his without a word, and took the shaft, and he fell back to the tail and walked with his hand on a box until the leg forgave him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:205:"You've left out the strap," said Brom, who had read none of it and knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:241:The valley ended. It did not close gently, as valleys did in the country below; the two hillsides simply drew together until they were walls, and between the walls there was a slot full of blue shadow, with the stream coming out of the bottom of it white. The far wall went up sheer and dark for as high as he could tip back his head. The near one, on their own side of the water, was broken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:39:"Does it do anything else? Tide. Could you fight with it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:71:She opened her eyes and stepped back, and she rubbed her hands together slowly, as a person does who has had them in cold water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:"That's three times. The first I thought it was the break; a bad one can shout over everything. So I went where you're whole. There's nothing wrong with that wrist, or that neck, and there's still nothing I can read." She looked at Karis, who was the one writing it down, or would be. "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. Something's moving. I can't find the banks, so I can't read it, so I can't touch it. That's the rule. I didn't make it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:77:"Everybody's got them. What moves in a person moves in a bed, the same bed all their life, and the shape of the bed is their Path. I put my hands on you and I felt the banks before I felt anything else, and I said Ember, and I was right. I'd know a Wind from a Skin with a glove on." She jerked her chin at the table. "In him it's moving, and it hasn't a side anywhere that I can find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:87:"I don't run it on a road. I've not the day to spare for it, and he's three ribs short of whole." She turned back to Cael, and for the first time since a rock he saw something in her face that was neither tiredness nor arithmetic. It was appetite. "When you're not broken, I run the long one. It won't read you either. I want to feel it not read you properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:129:"Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:149:There was bone. Bone answered to anyone; she had felt the breaks as a blind man feels a step. There was movement, past the bone. She was sure of that, and it was what kept her fingers stupid. A dead thing she could have understood. This was more alive than most, and it ran, and she had felt it running; and she could no more have said which way, or between what, than she could have drawn the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:Three ribs, broken on the seventy-eighth. Twenty-one days to knit, in anybody. By what she had felt under her hand they would be sound in him some days sooner. But she could not read him, and so she could not know it, and she had never in her life lifted a tape on a guess. It would be the ninety-ninth, then, by the rule. The long one wanted him whole and still, and it wanted the better part of a day out of her besides, so that the hundredth was the first morning on which she could run it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:219:They were hungry for this. She had spent her life reading what people were built like, and she had been proud of never meeting a body she could not go into. Now she had met one. She did not want to mend him; there was nothing to mend that a strip of linen could not see to. She wanted to lay both hands on him, whole and still, for as long as it took, and feel exactly how it was that he could not be read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:243:It was the bow's hip that he minded, and he put it first, since it was the cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:245:He had timed the second of his two slips to end at the upper hold on the eighty-first day. That was the day the healer's round should have brought her to its gate. He had said nothing of it to the bow; he had only found a day's more work on a byre roof that might have waited, and stayed. She had not come, and nobody at that gate could say why. On the hill she had given him no more than that she was late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:249:But it was a day he had spent and a thing he had hoped for, and he could not set it down against five names. Pike had struck a ford off his hold and given it to them; that had cost nothing, because he had chosen the pass himself. The healer's lateness was not theirs on any ground he had read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:253:He read the yard through the open gate as he went by it, without turning his head more than a walking man does.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:255:There was no cart. That was the first thing, and it was the largest. He had heard on the road that they had hired the smith's, and there were three boxes standing against the gate-house wall on the bare ground, and a crew that has wheels does not stand its goods on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:275:So he had it, and there was nothing in it that a man could not have read off a yard and a keeper. They had cleared a ford with five, in a way that nobody on those roads had cleared one before, and they had walked a mountain and brought back a true answer; and they had lost a cart doing it, and come down with three of them marked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:279:The first was the two jobs, set side by side with the same number on each. It would cost him nothing, and it would tell the boy that he was being read, and by what. That was fair. The boy had read him across a room and not troubled to hide it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:283:The third would land hardest, and he turned it over longest. He did not mean it unkindly. A cart was not a carter. Nobody lay in that gorge. But a crew that went out with another man's wheels and came back without them had done a thing that the board would enter, and it was better that they heard the word for it from him, on a river bank, than read it on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:287:He looked back, once. The spear. The boy. The bow, on the good leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:293:Teague still could not tell what he was. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and there was no Path in the set of that boy that a man could name, and no want of one. He went down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:321:They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:331:*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:333:He read the last of it over, and it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:"They're eating the hill," said Lira, close to his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:21:"The shelf does," said Cael. "For the next ten hundreds, by the look of it, and it's the only shelf."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:83:"The east end is mine," said Karis, "and you have the west. Lira takes whichever of them does something. Nobody is to tell me what he thinks it means; I want what it did, and when."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:85:The first observation was the spacing, and she had it within the hour. He counted the intervals for her in lengths, since there was no means of taping them, and between every animal and the next there was room for one of them and half another; a calf and its dam made one place, and the gap was kept from the pair. When the sixth from his end had scraped out what was in front of it and moved up a pace, the seventh did the same a breath afterward, and the eighth after that. He watched the gap close and open again down eleven animals, like a pulse going along a rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:113:"Not a head, and that went by as close to them as I am to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:159:"There wasn't. I've got good eyes, and I've watched a starting line since I was small. I know what it looks like when one goes and the rest follow, because you can see it run along the row, and this didn't run along." Lira sat up. "It was like one animal thirty carts long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:205:She wrote for some minutes, numbering her clauses as she had numbered them in a barn, and when she read the page out it was shorter than he had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:207:"*Shale-back. Seventy-fifth day. One: it does not answer fire at three hundred and fifty yards, nor sound, nor a blow on its own shelf. Two: it answers something given off by one of its own in fright, at once, the whole herd together, by moving away. Three: by that, and by the carters' rule, it will answer a declaration in the same way, and a declaration is a great deal larger than a frightened animal.*" She stopped. "*Derived. Not tested.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:219:She said it without any weight, as she would have read out a width.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:225:At the ford he had found a seam. He had sat on a gravel bar with four hurt people and gone over what he had counted, and it had yielded a first mover to give the pack, and a place to put a knife behind it. There was no seam in this. Nothing on that hill went first or could be led, and there was no cut dog; if those thirty ever ran, there would be no opponent to read, only a slope, and the single question of where a person ought to be standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:"The other one I gave you because I couldn't hold a pencil. This I can write myself, and I have. But it's derived, and a derived thing in one hand is an opinion." She was looking at the leaf, and not at Hesk's book, which she had never read. "You had the far end of the line. If you'll put down what you saw there, in your own words, then it's two people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:239:He sat down a little behind the rest and held out his hands to it, and he let a full minute go by before he spoke, which was his way of saying that the thing bore.

