"Are you a healer?" said Lira. Her teeth were knocking together. "A woman at a gate said there wasn't one. She said the nearest walked a route."

"Tide. Healer."

"She said the rest of you keep to the country where somebody ranks you."

"Most do."

"Do they rank you?"

The woman had laid two fingers on the shin, a hand's breadth below the knee, as lightly as on the string of an instrument.

"Iron. Four, if anybody's keeping a book up here."

"Nobody is," said Lira.

"I shall be," said a faint voice from under Seln's coat, "as soon as I can hold the pencil."

---

She took off the second slate, and then she put both hands round the leg and shut her eyes.

Her lips moved. Cael watched them, having nothing else to do, and found that she was counting; she went to ten, slowly, and stopped, and opened her eyes.

"It's broken through, clean, a hand below the knee," she said. "The one bone, and the other's whole. The ends have slipped by each other the width of my thumb. I have to bring them back to where they belong before I mend it, or I mend it short, and you walk on that for the rest of your life." She looked at Brom. "You. Behind her. Take her under the arms and don't let her come with me."

Brom got down on the floor and took Lira against his chest.

"That's the part you'll mind."

She said it to Lira, plainly, as a ferryman says that the next step is wet. Then she set one hand above the place and one at the ankle, over the dirty linen, and leaned back with her whole weight, steadily, as one draws a bucket.

Lira made one sound, and it was not loud. Thirty yards off, out on the level, a beaked head came up and went down again.

"There," said Oryn. "That's it over. You'll not get worse than that from me."

She did not let go. She shifted her hands until both of them lay round the shin where the wrong turn had been, with the fingers overlapping, and she settled on her heels, and her face went quiet.

There was nothing to see. He had half expected a light, or a colour, as there was grey in Iron Skin and yellow in a point; and there was a square brown woman kneeling in the dust with her hands on a girl's leg, breathing slowly. A minute went by, and another, and the only thing that changed was that the sweat came out on her forehead, though the air was cold.

"What are you doing?" said Karis. She had got up on one elbow. "I want it described, for I can't write, but I can remember till I can."

"Finding where it's stopped."

"Where what has?"

"What she mends with. Everybody's got it. It goes round you like water round a mill, and when you break a bone it does the knitting, if you leave it be for six weeks." Oryn spoke in pieces, between breaths, with her eyes shut. "At a break it stands, since it can't get by, and it piles up, and that's the heat you felt in this last night. I find where it's standing. Then I open the way with mine, and hers goes through, and I hold it open while it does the six weeks."

"All of the six weeks?"

"All of them. That's what I pay. It doesn't come from nowhere."

Lira's face went a colour that was not white, and she said "Cold," not to anyone, and then "No. It's going along," and put her head back and shut her eyes.

"That's right," said Oryn. "Lie still and let it."

It took the greater part of an hour, and he counted it by the shadow of the wall, which crept the width of two hands across the floor. After the first of it the hollow was silent. Brom held Lira as he would have held a full pail, and Seln stood at the mule's head with the rein in his fingers; and Cael watched a stranger's face, and saw it grow a little older while he looked.

When she took her hands away, the shin was straight.

Lira lifted her head to see it. She moved the toes of her right foot, in the torn stocking, all five.
"He'll go to draw a full breath, or turn to pick something up, and that pulls before the bone does, so he stops there. He'll have learned it by tonight, and he'll hate it by noon tomorrow, and it stays on till I take it off." She looked at him at last. "Twenty-one days from the morning they went, and I want to be the one who lifts it. Can you count that far?"

"He can count anything," said Brom. "It's stopping him that's the work."

"Why tape for him," said Karis slowly, "and hands for her? You had two mendings yesterday and you've two this morning, and a rib is a bone like a shin."

Cael had been waiting for that question, and he saw that the others had too, for Lira's eyes came up from her leg.

"Because I cannot mend what I can't read." The healer said it without heat. "I read her leg at a count of ten and I knew what it was, and how she was built round it, and where the thing that knits had stopped; and then I knew where to put mine. I had my hands on him for three times as long and I got bone. I'd have got that much with my eyes shut, off a dead sheep." She showed her palms, briefly. "Past the bone there's nothing I can lay hold of. I'd be pushing in the dark, and I don't do that to anybody."

She turned to Brom then, and had his left arm out of its sleeve before he could offer it. The seam was dry and pale. She wound a strip of plain linen round the wrist, four turns, no tighter than a cuff, and tied it.

"That's not holding anything either. It's closed, and it's sound, and for a week it's the weakest thing on you, so this is to remind the hand." She tapped the cloth. "It'll take what sits on it. It won't take what hangs from it. No rope, no rail, and no hauling anybody up anything; and if you find it shut on something that's pulling away from you, let go."

"A week from yesterday, at the middle of the day," said Brom. "I'll know the hour."

Ground, not teeth. The first true freedom is a paid catalogue of a mountain; the mountain throws them anyway; and the sixth chair walks up a lip as a professional to a patient she cannot read. Turns and promises:

- **The Oxhollow slip** (seventy, on the map, paid at Oxhollow) sorts itself to the top, and he counts why: it pays; nobody's finger is on it; it is *ground*. Pike: take a cart ("You want to tell a ford it holds a cart, you'd better have had one on it."). Seln asks about shale-backs quietly at the plank's end, and Cael does not go to listen. The smith's cart is six against its return. The boxes go because there is nowhere to leave them.
- **The fan and the bench, given room (≈7,000):** a cart hauled on a rope; the shelf pitched like a roof; the gorge that gives every sound back late. Lira runs in hundreds; Karis's cord, width and pitch, stones dropped and counted; Brom says a cart on scree "floats" and walks against the strap; Seln is always already where they will sleep. Cael is paid by the line for the first time in his life. **Holding still applies:** he uses nothing on the mountain.
- **The herd and Karis's day** as a full learning encounter: spacing; the line moved without an animal seen moving; a rock passing between two of them (a step, not flight); the plate going and the herd stepping uphill *as one, no first animal*. Laid beside the ford page: same sense, opposite answer. "Derived. Not tested." "On a slope, flight is a landslide with legs." "Write it in yours, too. I'd like it in two hands."
- **The narrowings** and the eighth's clean break. Brom takes the cart across it, and the tail-board doesn't bang because nobody lets it. The ledge in sight. "I'll run it at first light."
- **The ledge and the stampede (≈4,500, terrain fight):** the ledge goes under Lira; her own Wind, full, crosses the gorge; thirty heads come up; the shelf cannot hold them at a run, and they come down the face in a front. "There. Thirty seconds. Lee side." "Cart's gone." **Karis's lattice line** across the face is a declaration the size of a road. The herd splits round the fin at eleven paces; she sits down at the eighth second, emptied. Brom goes back for two boxes (the wrist goes under the cloth). Cael goes after the third. Compression and Anchor *want*, and he prices both and holds, because the split stands on Karis's line and one more loud thing turns it. He takes the shoulder raw: three ribs, left side, low; the calf opened on the shale. The cart floats and goes in with its shafts up. Lira's leg broken under a plate, the boot gone. "That was me… The loud thing." / "It was the ledge. The ledge went. Then it was you." / "Then it was me."
- **The fin's lee, days 78–79, on the page:** rationing; the lip walked by Seln; the fan still sliding; Lira's leg hot under the plates; Karis unable to light a lamp; nobody writes.
- **Day 80, Oryn**, introduced through her walk and then her hands: "Who's worst?"; the set ("That's the part you'll mind"); the mend in less than an hour (Lira: "Cold… No. It's going along."); she sits as a carter sets down a known load; "That's one."; "Hands on. That's the only way it works… And I can't do it to me."; Brom's wrist ("Then it should have closed in a month and hasn't, because you've pulled on it."); Karis: "Ember. And nothing. You're empty."; "Hm." at the lattice line. **The first failed surface reading** on Cael: she stops in the middle, looks at her hands, tries again, and her face does the instrument's thing. The protected exchange ends on "That's a *finding*."
- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
- **Oxhollow (day 84):** the table under the lean-to, hers twenty days at a time; worst first; "That's three." The map paid seventy ("Then they go round."). **The second and third readings** (ribs knitting with "nothing doing it"; the wrist and neck, nothing wrong, still nothing): "a river in the dark." **The two readings taught before they are load-bearing** (surface: a count of ten; deep: both hands, minutes, still and whole, at a mending's cost; "I don't run it on a road"). If the stall is named (C8 default: at most once), it is a limit she prices and hates, never used. **Three questions** (right places — yes; faster; no healer ever got anything — no). "That's the visit." He has a sentence asking her south and does not say it. "Thornwater's on my route in nine days… I want to read you when you're not broken." / "Nine days."
- **Teague at Oxhollow's ford at dusk:** four lines (protected), not unkind; Cael has no argument. The log. The image: Teague's four walking the ford quiet, and a mule a mile up the north road with a woman on its down-slope side.

## What must remain true / withheld

- **No fragment used** on the mountain. Compression and Anchor want, and he holds. Deployment since the Line stays **one**.
- Oryn knows nothing of fragments; no deep reading is run. She records nothing and remembers. Her Tide in full: contact only; about three serious mendings a day, fed and slept (two on a road); cannot heal herself; "cannot mend what I can't read."
- The herd's flight is **derived**, not tested. The herd's dead are "heard, not sworn."
- **The ankle is not mended.** The leg is.
- Nobody mentions quiet ground. Oryn's other five holds are not named, here or in her window.
- Teague learns nothing he couldn't read off a yard.

## What the author owns

The bench's days and weather; the herd's look and sound; the exact choreography inside the planted geometry (fin, split, cart, boxes); Oryn's first look at each of them; the walk down; the yard at Oxhollow; which patients come to the table; Lira's and Oryn's windows; humor (Brom's "Carrying." / "Not pulling."; Lira on a mule not mentioning the mule).


