books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:17:And the third reason, which he counted last because it was his: it was ground. Not teeth. A scouting run was a thing walked and measured and written down and handed to a man who would trust a cart to the writing, and he had done that every day of his life since he was fourteen, in a notebook, for nothing, at the edges of rooms where the work being paid for was somebody else's. He had never once been paid to catalogue. He read the slip a third time to be sure. It was what it said.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:31:The cart was the smith's second-best, two wheels and a bed and a pair of shafts with a chest-strap between them. Brom stood in the shafts and put the strap across his chest and leaned on it once, and it moved, and he nodded, and that was the cart's whole trial. The bound arm went inside the right shaft against his ribs. The good hand took the left.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:41:Lira's ankle: the stick was gone, leaned against the inn wall on the sixty-fourth day and walked away from. What she had instead was cloth — the steading's boiled linen cut into strips, wound each morning from the arch up over the joint and tied off at the shin, tight, so that the ankle bent as far as the cloth allowed and no further, which was less than the other, and later. She called it taping. She did it before her boots, sitting, with her mouth shut.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:49:Two days up the Thornwater road to the fan, with the cart; the stretch walked at noon, quiet. They did not go up to Thornwater's gate. The slide had come down a mile short of the steading, and the road went over the toe of what it had left and the traverse went up its side, and there was a cairn, knocked half over, where the two parted. Seln set the stones back on it while the rest of them looked up.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:59:He wrote that down first, at the first halt, because it was the fact everything else stood on. A shelf of grey shale along the mountain's face, a cart and a half wide where they came onto it and sloping toward the outer edge as a roof slopes — not steep, but not flat, so that anything set down on it wanted to go one way. On the left the mountain went up in the same stone, plated, and two hundred meters up, by the eye, a second shelf ran along it, greener than theirs: an upper bench. On the right the shelf stopped, and past where it stopped there was nothing for a long way down, and at the bottom of the nothing a white line that was a river.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:65:She had taped the ankle at the fan's foot and again at the top, tighter, and then she went out along the bench at a run — not a burst; a burst was a shout, she had learned that on a gravel bar with seven dogs turning toward her — a hundred paces out over the shale with nothing under it but her, and came back at a walk, reading the ground with her feet. Shale that held a walker held nothing, she said; shale that held a runner held a wheel. She ran the bench in hundreds all afternoon and the cart followed in her prints, and at the evening halt she sat down before anyone and took the boot off and did not look at the ankle, and then looked at it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:69:She had paced a length of the void road against the folding rule on the first morning out of Lowmarch and had a number for her stride she trusted; now she walked the bench counting, the pen in the scarred hand, and at every place where the shelf narrowed she ran the cord across it edge to edge and wrote the width, and the count to it from the last width, and the pitch, which she took with the cord and a stone hung on it. Where the gorge came close she dropped a stone over and counted until the sound came up. What went into the book was a chain of numbers with a drawing beside it that looked like nothing at all — a line, kinked, with figures along it — and would look, to a man at Oxhollow with a cart, like the only true thing on the mountain.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:73:He said, at the second halt, that a cart on scree did not roll; it floated. The wheels sat on plates that sat on plates and the whole of it went where the slope wanted, which was over the edge, a finger's width at every turn of the wheel, unless the weight was over the axle and up-slope. He re-roped the boxes at every halt to keep the front one where it was, took the down-slope shaft himself as the counter, and walked the whole day with the cart's want against his chest, and it was — Cael watched for the word — a lock somebody else had built, being held shut by hand.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:103:The colour of the shale, which was why he had not seen it. Then, because he knew what he was looking for, the next one, and the next, and he counted them as he counted a crowd, left to right and again, and got twenty-nine and then thirty-one and then thirty, and kept thirty. Thirty animals along the upper bench, every one of them with its face down against the rock. Plated across the back in slabs that overlapped like the stone of a roof, grey going to rust as the shale went; the head small and low for the body and the legs short and thick under it; each one, by the eye at that distance, the size of the cart with its load and Brom in the shafts. They were eating lichen — the green was lichen, on the upper shelf where the wind did not scour it — and the sound he had taken for the gorge since the narrowing was them: a grinding, patient and low, thirty sets of teeth on stone, coming down to the lower bench and then back from the far wall, late.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:133:He sat a length from her with his own book and catalogued the herd as he would have catalogued a fighter he was going to have to watch for a season: how they stood; what they did with the weight; what a head did before a body moved. Thirty animals, and not one of them made a movement in an hour that he would have called a decision. Lira had the day too, on the cart's tail with the boot off and the ankle in the sun.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:195:Six, seven, eight — Karis's cord at each, and the widths coming down: a cart and a half, a cart and a hand, a cart. At the eighth the outer edge was not crumbling, it was gone, a clean break where a length of the shelf had let go some season past; Lira ran it first, twice, and came back and said *it holds a runner* and did not say the rest, and Brom took the cart across it with the down-slope wheel a hand from nothing and his whole weight on the up-slope shaft and his face shut, and it went across, and the tail-board did not bang, because nobody let it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:201:The fourth camp was an overhang again, because Seln found overhangs. The fire was small. Lira taped the ankle by it, as she did at night now as well as in the morning, and nobody said anything about it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:217:Karis stood with the cord in her hand and did not run it across anything. "I can't measure that from here. Morning."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:225:She did it as she had done it every night on the bench: the day's cloth unwound and laid across her knee, the ankle turned in the firelight as far as it would turn, which was less than the other one, and later; and then the night's strip, from the arch up over the joint and off at the shin, pulled tight, tied. It was the ankle that had never been seen by anyone who could do more for it than boil the cloth. She had not stopped taping it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:11:They had walked the last of the bench in the dark for that. She had taped the ankle at camp by feel, put the boot on over it, and said nothing about either. Now she stood where the shelf became a lip along the mountain's bulge: a cart wide for ten paces, then less, then a ledge the width of a door running out over nothing toward where the bench opened again and went on to Oxhollow. Below it the gorge was wider than anywhere they had seen it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:13:Brom had the strap on and the cart's tail a length back from the ledge's root, the three boxes roped at its front, the down-slope wheel a hand from the edge. Seln was not looking at the ledge but back along the bench, at a thing Cael had walked past in the dark and catalogued as the one object on the traverse that was not a plate: a fin of darker stone standing out of the mountain at the bench's inner edge, forty paces behind them, a man high and three carts long. Seln looked at it as he looked at the places they would sleep.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:17:Not a run; she read it with her feet first, ten paces at a walk with a hand on the mountain, then the hand off and the walk going to the thing she did between a walk and a run, the ankle taking it. The ledge held a walker. At thirty meters she turned to come back at a run, because shale that held a runner held a wheel, and that was the word Oxhollow was paying for.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:43:The fin came up. He got into the shale in its lee with his shoulder to the stone and Seln's hand on his collar putting him lower, and Brom came in over him, and Karis did not come in. She stopped at the fin's up-slope end, in the open, back to the rock, face to the mountain, and put both scarred hands up in front of her.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:59:The front came down the face at a gallop with the mountain under it and met a line it had to turn from, and it turned, as water turns at a stone, half left and half right, at a distance he measured afterward at eleven paces from the fin's face and could not have measured then, because there was no distance; there was a grey wall going past his shoulder at the length of his arm with plates the size of doors coming off it. The noise was inside the stone at his back. The down-slope half had nothing under its outer feet but the bench and then the edge, and some of it went over. He heard that: a different sound, and the far wall not giving it back. Most of it did not, and turned along the bench, and went.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:95:The second half came past it, and the cart did what Brom had said a cart did on scree, which was float: a finger's width at every turn of the wheel, and then not a finger's width. It went sideways along the bench in the shale moving under the animals, and its down-slope wheel went out over where the bench was no longer, and it tipped as if considering it, and went into the gorge with the shafts up.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:107:She had gone for the fin when they went for it, on the ankle that had gone at the ford and gone again when she landed; he pieced that afterward from the marks in the shale. What had come down on her had not been an animal. It had been the bench: a plate going out under her foot as the second half came down and the whole shelf shifted, the leg going into the gap it left, and the plate above coming down on the leg with the mountain behind it. The plate was still there. Seln lifted it off. Under it the leg below the knee was the wrong shape. Not bent; the wrong shape, a line with an angle in it where there was not supposed to be an angle. The foot had no boot. The ledge had taken the boot. A strip of tape was still round the ankle above the bare foot, tied off at the shin.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:109:She did not make a sound coming in. Seln carried her most of it. She looked at the leg as she had looked at the ankle at every fire on the bench: turned it in her head as far as it would turn, and then looked.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:117:"Then it was me." Lira put her head back against the stone.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:125:Lira: the right leg broken below the knee, the shape wrong and the skin whole, the foot bare, the ankle under the break gone twice and no longer the largest thing in the leg. Seln straightened it as far as it would go, with Brom holding her and her own hand in her mouth, and it was not straight. Two plates of the mountain went either side of it, and Karis's cord round the plates from knee to ankle, tight, so that the leg was a thing in a box. She did not faint, and he had never seen her work so hard at anything.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:141:The map: in Karis's book, in Karis's satchel, on Karis. Nine narrowings and their widths, and now a tenth, which was not a narrowing. It was an absence. The ledge was gone for a cart's width along three carts' length; what remained was a lip against the bulge that a walker could take with her hands on the rock, and a mule could not, and Oxhollow's carts would not, ever. Seln walked out along the lip in the afternoon and came back at dusk and said it went; that beyond the bulge the bench opened as they had seen it; that for a walker Oxhollow was two days on.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:157:The next day was the one the mountain kept. Seln went at first light with the pack on and came back before the sun was over the gorge: the lip held a walker, and below the lip the fan the herd had taken down was still going, in slides, one every hundred breaths, and a man on it was a stone on it. "Tomorrow," he said, "or it's two of us." The day was rationing and the leg. Karis woke and could not light a lamp and did not try again. Lira's leg went hot under the plates by noon, and the tape came off and went back on wetted, and she said nothing about it, which was how Cael knew. He counted breaths, and the slides on the fan, and the gorge, and did not take the log out.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:165:There was a mule on the bench.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:173:"Who's worst?"
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:177:"Name's Oryn." She said it to the splint. "Tide. Healer." She had the cord off the plates and the tape off the ankle before anyone moved to help her. "A carter came into Oxhollow off the void road two days back at noon and said the shale-backs had come off the mountain onto the low road at a run, and the crew that was up on the traverse was overdue off it. I was at the ford." She looked up, at Lira. "When."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:189:"Iron. Four, if anybody's keeping a book up here." She did not look at him. "Broken through. Both bones. Two days old. Somebody's ankle under it has been wrong for a month." She said it to Lira as a list. "I'm going to set it. That's the part you'll mind. Then I'm going to mend it, and you'll mind that less. Big man: knee. You" — Seln — "shoulders. Both hands."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:203:She had both hands on the leg, one above the break and one below, and the nearest word he had was that she was pouring, without anything moving. Lira's face went a colour that was not white, and she said "Cold," not to anyone, and then "No. It's going along," and put her head back and shut her eyes. The swelling at the break went down, not as a swelling goes down over a night but as water goes out of a basin when the plug is pulled, in the time it takes to watch, and the skin over the place lay flat, and the two lines of Lira's shin, the wrong one and the right one, were one line, and stayed one line, and Oryn's hands came off it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:209:"That's one," she said. To nobody. She drank from a skin, a long time. Then, to Lira: "Weight on it tomorrow. Not today. The bone's whole; it's new. It'll ache for a season and hold for the rest of your life. The ankle under it I've not touched; I've not got it in me this morning, and it's not going to kill you. Somebody tape it. You'll know how."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:245:"I'll be the one who says that." She knelt in the shale at his side and did not ask him to move, and put both hands on him, one flat on the ribs where the shoulder had taken him, one on his back behind it, and went still.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:11:Seln went first, one hand on the mountain, and stood at the far end where the bench opened and the mule was, and waited. Lira went second. The bone was whole, Oryn had said, and new, and today it took weight; she stood up off the shale on it with her face doing nothing and the bare foot under the taped ankle going grey in the cold, and she walked the lip as she had walked the ledge, feet first, reading it, slower, with a hand on the rock the whole way. Oryn went behind her. Not close. A stride and a half — near enough to take a hand, far enough not to be taken by one; and Cael, who had catalogued where people stood since he was fourteen, catalogued it. She had put herself where a fall would come. Not in front, where Seln was, and not at the back, where Brom would be. Where the hurt would arrive, if it arrived.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:13:The boxes went next, one at a time, on backs. Brom put the strap of the first over his shoulders and Oryn looked at the mended arm inside his sleeve and said "Carrying," and Brom said "Not pulling," and she said "Hm," and it stood. Seln came back for the second. The third went on the mule.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:17:The mule stood where it had stood for two days, tied to nothing. It looked at six people and three boxes as a mule looks at anything, and put its head down.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:23:He wrote that at the first halt, because he had the log and the satchel and nothing else — the first time since the Line that he had come off a job carrying nothing but paper — and the sentence was the shape of the thing he was in. Four days: the bench going down and widening and losing its edge until it was a slope and then a hillside; the shale giving way to turf in patches, and then to turf with shale in it; the gorge opening below into a valley with Oxhollow's river at the bottom of it. Lira on the mule, the bare foot in a sock of Oryn's linen, saying nothing about being on a mule. Brom under a box. Seln under a box, ahead, and then not ahead, and then ahead again. Karis with the packs and the map, drawing the bench down as she had drawn it up. And the healer.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:27:She walked like a carter: the pace set once at the start of a road and not changed for a slope or a narrow or anything short of an animal in the road; the feet going down whole and staying down; the weight never once ahead of them. Lira moved as if the ground were a thing she could leave. Oryn moved as if it were a thing she had an arrangement with. She was never fast. In four days he did not see her hurry, and he was watching for it — at the mule's stumbles, at the place on the second day where the ground went out from under Brom's box — and she came to each at the same walk, and was there when she was needed and not before. Item. She walked on the down-slope side of the mule, where a rider would come off. Item. She looked at the four of them who could walk as Seln looked at a road, every halt, once, all together, and then at whichever one had changed. Item. She had a heel gone raw in her boot from the mountain, and at the first halt she took the boot off and washed it and taped it with the same grey cloth she had put round his ribs, and put the boot back on, and walked. She did not put her hands on it and go still. She could not do that to herself. She had said so on the bench, and here it was, in a boot.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:29:Lira asked her about the ankle at the second day's noon.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:31:They had stopped where the slope flattened into turf, and Lira sat against a stone with the leg out, and said it as she said everything, as a fact for checking: "You could do the ankle."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:37:"Three." Oryn had her skin out and was drinking, not much. "Three a day, if I'm fed and I've slept, and that's a good day; two's more like it on a road. That's what I've got, all told, for everybody I put my hands on between here and the next place I sleep." She capped the skin. "I spent three on your mountain. A leg, a wrist, and a leg's worth of walking up. Oxhollow was owed a visit six days ago and I was on a bench instead. Somebody at Oxhollow's got worse in six days. I don't know who yet. There's always someone." She looked at the ankle, once. "That's a tape and a season. It'll set. Somebody at the ford's a leg, or a chest, or a child. Your ankle's not going to be why they didn't get me."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:39:Lira looked at the ankle, and then at the healer, and said, "Right." She did not ask again. He had known her four years and could count on a hand the things she had asked for twice.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:43:At the same halt Oryn came round the stone to him and said "Calf," and he gave it to her: Seln's binding, four days old and stiff with the mountain, and the wound under it, the ford's two mouths opened along the shale and closed again, mostly, as things closed on him. She looked at it as a carter looks at a wheel. She washed it from her skin and bound it fresh in her cloth, tight, and did not put her hands on it and go still.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:57:"Twenty days," Oryn said. "Round. Two at a hold, near enough, and the road between, and the road's the part that doesn't move. A hold can wait a day. The road can't be walked faster than it's walked." She had the mule's lead in her hand and was working a burr out of it. "I've walked it round thirty-odd times. I've been late to a hold eleven of them. This is twelve."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:61:"Oxhollow shoes the mule and keeps a table I don't have to carry. Thornwater feeds me. One of the five gave me this coat two winters back and won't hear about it." She did not look up from the burr. "And I owe them. There's a hand at Oxhollow I said I'd look at again and haven't. There's a child three stops on that had a fever the day I left, and I don't know. That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:69:"I remember who I've had my hands on," she said. "That's the book. A healer who needs a page to know who's owed has stopped looking at them."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:87:It was hoof-cut and plated for a hundred paces where the herd had come off the fan and crossed it — shale on turf, as if the mountain had walked a piece of the way to Oxhollow and thought better of it — and then it was a road again, and empty. Seln read the ground and said twenty-seven, in a voice that meant the carter had been right. Karis lit the fire that night: one point, on the first try, the first thing she had lit since the road, and sat down after it, not hard. Oryn looked at her and said "Eat," and Karis ate.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:89:The fourth day was river and turf and the valley opening; and Oxhollow, in the afternoon, at the gorge's mouth.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:91:He read it as he read a board. A river coming out of the mountain grey and fast and going brown as it slowed; a ford where a gravel bar split it, the water over the bar the height of a knee and moving; on the near bank, under the last of the bench, a stockade of grey poles the height of a man and a gate in it standing open; inside, eleven roofs and a yard, carts drawn up nose to tail along the water, mules, the smell of wet rope and dung and something cooking; and against the stockade's inside face, by the gate, a lean-to with a table under it and nothing on the table.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:95:There were already people at it. Someone had seen the mule come down the road.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:97:"You're last," Oryn said to the five of them, at the yard's edge, and it was not unkind; it was the order. "You're the only ones I've had my hands on this month. Sit." And went to the table at the walk she had come down the mountain at, and the line closed round her.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:99:He saw it from the yard, which was as near as he went; it was hers, not a crew's. An old man with a chest that whistled. A girl with a burn up one arm that had been dressed by somebody who did not know how. A carter with a hand under a cloth — the hand, Cael thought, that she had said she would look at again. She took them in the order they were worst, not the order they had come, and nobody in the line argued the order, which told him how long the table had been hers. She went still over the chest for the seconds and then for the quarter-hour, and the whistle stopped. The girl's arm took the time an apple takes. The carter's hand she unwrapped and looked at for a long while and put both hands on, and said something to the man, low, and he nodded and went; and Cael did not know whether that had been the third until she sat down on the bench behind the table and drank, with the grey in her face, and said, to nobody, "That's three," and the line, which knew what that meant, thinned to the ones who only wanted looking at.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:115:"Six days," she said. "Those feel like a fortnight. They're knitting. They're knitting as bone knits, only faster than bone does, and there's nothing doing it. No current. Nothing I can feel going along them and telling them to." Her hands came off. "They're doing it themselves. I've had my hands on a hundred ribs. Ribs don't."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:121:Lira put the leg on the bench, and Oryn put both hands on it above and below where the angle had been, and went still, and it was over in the seconds. "Whole. New. It aches." Lira said it did. "That's the right amount of ache. Don't run on it for a week. You will. Don't." She did not look at the ankle. "Arm."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:145:"Three things," she said, "and then I've a mule to load. When you heal — when you've done it on your own, the way you say you always have — does it hurt in the right places?"
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:159:"That's the visit," Oryn said, and stood up, which took her one try, and went to the mule.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:163:She loaded it herself, at the gate, with the sun going off the bench above the roofs. The roll under the strap; the panniers, which had carried a box for four days and now carried the kit. Lira, on two feet with a hand on the stockade, watched her and said nothing; Brom held the mule's lead while she tightened a girth and gave it back without being asked, and she said "Hm," to him, and it was thanks. It was not a leaving that had been arranged. It was a healer at the end of a stop.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:167:"Oxhollow feeds the mule. That's Oxhollow's." She took the lead, and looked at him, and it was the look from the third reading, the one with no number in it and no awe. "Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:169:He had a sentence. He had had it since the second night's fire, and it was a good sentence, and it asked her to walk south with five people instead of north with a mule, and he did not say it. He catalogued not saying it; that was the whole of what he did with it. She had seven holds and a child she did not know about. He had a board. She had told him at a fire what she owned, and it was not a thing you asked a person to set down for a crew that had known her four days.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:179:He heard the cart before he saw it, and the first thing he catalogued was that it was a cart. Two wheels, a bed, a load under canvas, and it was not in a gorge. Four with it. The tall woman walked ahead with the spear across her shoulders and her hands hung over it. The broad man with the crossbow and the limp had the cart's tail. The young one was between the shafts, pulling, and pulling it well. And Teague came last, as he had come last through Lowmarch's door, and stopped at the yard's edge where the water began and did the thing Cael had watched him do once: he looked at the woman, and at the man with the limp, and at the young one at the shafts, each for the length of a breath, and only when he had them did he look at the yard, and the yard had five people in it he had read across a room thirty-five days ago and not spoken to.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:195:He did not wait to be answered. It had not been a question. He went back across the yard at the same pace and said something short to the tall woman, and the young one let the shafts down, and the man with the limp hung the crossbow on the cart, and the four of them stood at the water and let the mule drink and did not look at the five of them again, because the ledger had been read and there was nothing else on the page.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:205:Across the yard the young one picked the shafts up. The tall woman went into the water first, with the spear across her shoulders, and the cart went in after her with the broad man at its tail, and Teague last; and the four of them walked Oxhollow's ford at dusk with the river to their knees and the cart's wheels finding the bar, and nobody spoke, and nobody splashed, and the crossbow hung where the man could reach it without looking. They walked it the way carters walked the void roads. Quiet. A crew that had walked this a hundred times and had never once lost the count of itself in the water. They came up onto the far bank and the cart came up after them, streaming, and the south road took them, and the light went off the river.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:207:He turned on the bank. Behind him, above the roofs, where the north road climbed along under the bench, there was one more thing in the last of the light, and he found it because he was looking. A mile up, by the eye. A mule, and a woman walking on the down-slope side of it at a pace that had not changed since the gate, small against the bench, and then smaller, going the way the route went.
"You sat up to watch me come in," she said. "So you weren't worst. Coat."

He got it open with one hand. She knelt on his left, and her fingers went in under the shirt and lay along his side, low, where the three small sounds had been; they were warm and very steady, and her lips began to move.

He counted with her. *One. Two.* It was the first time since he crossed the Line that anybody had laid a hand on him to learn what he was. *Three. Four.*

At five the reading stopped.

Her left hand stayed where it was. The right she put out behind her, without turning her head.

"Wrist," she said. "Anybody's."

It was Brom who gave her one, the left, with its new seam. She closed two fingers on it, and her lips moved as far as three, and she let it go.

"That's there," she said, to nobody.

She brought the hand back and laid it on his side beside the other, with the fingers of both spread wide, and shut her eyes; and this time she did not count at all.

He watched her face, and he knew what it was doing, for he had seen a brass plate in a district hall wait eleven seconds over a boy of fourteen before it lit. He had seen assessors look at a panel with that same small stillness, and a hooded seat, and a clerk with a form that had no box for him. It was what an instrument looked like when it reached for the thing it was made to read, and closed on air.

She was not an instrument, and that was the difference. She took her hands away at last and sat back on her heels, and she looked straight at him.

"Your ribs are broken," she said. "I can feel that they're broken. I can't feel *you*."

Behind her nobody was eating any longer. Lira had come up on both elbows, and Brom sat with the bread halfway to his mouth; Seln's mild eyes were on the back of the healer's cropped head, and under his coat Karis had gone perfectly still.

"Whatever you are, I can't read it."

He waited for the next thing, since he knew the order in which it came. First the instrument failed, and then somebody asked him what he was, and wrote the answer down, and the answer became a word on a file.

The question did not come.
*Eightieth day. Lira's leg is straight. The boxes are ours. The cart is in the gorge. A healer came up the road because a carter said we were late.*

He closed it. She was standing over him.

---

"You," she said. "Ribs. I saw you breathe."

"Three. Left side. Low."

"I'll be the one who says that." She knelt in the shale at his side and did not ask him to move, and put both hands on him, one flat on the ribs where the shoulder had taken him, one on his back behind it, and went still.

Seconds. He counted them. He watched her face because it was in front of him: the face of a woman listening for a thing she had heard a thousand times, as a fighter listens for a step behind him.

She stopped.

Not as she had stopped at the leg or the arm, at the end of the seconds, with the finding ready. She stopped in the middle of them. Her hands came off him. She looked at them, both, palm and back, as a man looks at a tool that has done a thing it has never done. Then she put them back, exactly where they had been, and went still again, and held it longer, and her face did a thing.

He had watched that thing on faces for four years. At fourteen, for eleven seconds, from the inside. On assessors' faces at Ardenmere and at Greyvane, and on a panel in the academy above Ostrand, from the outside, over a page with his name on it and a bracket after it. It was the face of an instrument that has been put to a thing and has returned nothing, not a wrong number, no number, and is running itself again to be sure. He had never seen it on a healer. Healers did not read the page. Healers read the leg.

She took her hands off him and did not look at them this time. She looked at him.

"Your ribs are broken," she said. "I can feel that they're broken. I can't feel *you*."

Nobody moved. Lira's eyes had opened. Karis, against the box, did not move anything but her eyes.

"Whatever you are, I can't read it." It was said as the leg had been said: a list, exact, at the pace of a woman who did not hurry a finding because the finding was bad. "I've never said that to anyone. Tape them. They'll heal on their own." A breath. "They always have, haven't they?"

"Yes," he said.

"That's not an answer," Oryn said. "That's a *finding*."
# Chapter 7 — The Healer's Route

She taped his ribs on the eighty-first morning, before the sun was on the bench, and told him what it was while she did it.

"This is cloth," Oryn said. "Anybody can do cloth. Breathe out. All of it." She took the linen round him under the arm and over the place three times, pulling at each turn until the breath he had left stopped where she wanted it and not where the shale-back had decided. "There. Now it stops at the tape. That's the whole of what tape does. It doesn't mend anything. It tells the ribs where the edge is so they stop looking for it." She tied it off at the front, flat, with the knot where a strap would not lie. "You'll hate it by noon. Leave it."

He breathed in and the breath stopped a finger's width sooner than it had for three days, at a place he could feel from the outside, and it was better. He did not say so. She had not asked.

Then the lip.

Seln went first, one hand on the mountain, and stood at the far end where the bench opened and the mule was, and waited. Lira went second. The bone was whole, Oryn had said, and new, and today it took weight; she stood up off the shale on it with her face doing nothing and the bare foot under the taped ankle going grey in the cold, and she walked the lip as she had walked the ledge, feet first, reading it, slower, with a hand on the rock the whole way. Oryn went behind her. Not close. A stride and a half — near enough to take a hand, far enough not to be taken by one; and Cael, who had catalogued where people stood since he was fourteen, catalogued it. She had put herself where a fall would come. Not in front, where Seln was, and not at the back, where Brom would be. Where the hurt would arrive, if it arrived.

The boxes went next, one at a time, on backs. Brom put the strap of the first over his shoulders and Oryn looked at the mended arm inside his sleeve and said "Carrying," and Brom said "Not pulling," and she said "Hm," and it stood. Seln came back for the second. The third went on the mule.

Cael went last, with Seln behind him, because Seln had come back a third time without being asked. Thirty paces of the mountain's edge with nothing under the outer foot; a hand on the rock and the next foot, and the breath stopping at the tape, and the calf saying what it had said since the shale. He counted the paces because there was nothing else to count, and got thirty-one, and stepped off onto the wide bench, and the mountain was behind them.

The mule stood where it had stood for two days, tied to nothing. It looked at six people and three boxes as a mule looks at anything, and put its head down.

---

Four days down.

He wrote that at the first halt, because he had the log and the satchel and nothing else — the first time since the Line that he had come off a job carrying nothing but paper — and the sentence was the shape of the thing he was in. Four days: the bench going down and widening and losing its edge until it was a slope and then a hillside; the shale giving way to turf in patches, and then to turf with shale in it; the gorge opening below into a valley with Oxhollow's river at the bottom of it. Lira on the mule, the bare foot in a sock of Oryn's linen, saying nothing about being on a mule. Brom under a box. Seln under a box, ahead, and then not ahead, and then ahead again. Karis with the packs and the map, drawing the bench down as she had drawn it up. And the healer.

He catalogued her walk on the first day, because it was the first thing she did in front of him that was not her hands.

She walked like a carter: the pace set once at the start of a road and not changed for a slope or a narrow or anything short of an animal in the road; the feet going down whole and staying down; the weight never once ahead of them. Lira moved as if the ground were a thing she could leave. Oryn moved as if it were a thing she had an arrangement with. She was never fast. In four days he did not see her hurry, and he was watching for it — at the mule's stumbles, at the place on the second day where the ground went out from under Brom's box — and she came to each at the same walk, and was there when she was needed and not before. Item. She walked on the down-slope side of the mule, where a rider would come off. Item. She looked at the four of them who could walk as Seln looked at a road, every halt, once, all together, and then at whichever one had changed. Item. She had a heel gone raw in her boot from the mountain, and at the first halt she took the boot off and washed it and taped it with the same grey cloth she had put round his ribs, and put the boot back on, and walked. She did not put her hands on it and go still. She could not do that to herself. She had said so on the bench, and here it was, in a boot.

Lira asked her about the ankle at the second day's noon.

They had stopped where the slope flattened into turf, and Lira sat against a stone with the leg out, and said it as she said everything, as a fact for checking: "You could do the ankle."

"I could."

"But."

"Three." Oryn had her skin out and was drinking, not much. "Three a day, if I'm fed and I've slept, and that's a good day; two's more like it on a road. That's what I've got, all told, for everybody I put my hands on between here and the next place I sleep." She capped the skin. "I spent three on your mountain. A leg, a wrist, and a leg's worth of walking up. Oxhollow was owed a visit six days ago and I was on a bench instead. Somebody at Oxhollow's got worse in six days. I don't know who yet. There's always someone." She looked at the ankle, once. "That's a tape and a season. It'll set. Somebody at the ford's a leg, or a chest, or a child. Your ankle's not going to be why they didn't get me."

Lira looked at the ankle, and then at the healer, and said, "Right." She did not ask again. He had known her four years and could count on a hand the things she had asked for twice.

He put the arithmetic on the shelf beside Vell's. It was the same shape. A finite thing, kept in a head, spent by a rule the keeper had not made and would not break, and the rule was not who deserved it. It was who was next on the road.

At the same halt Oryn came round the stone to him and said "Calf," and he gave it to her: Seln's binding, four days old and stiff with the mountain, and the wound under it, the ford's two mouths opened along the shale and closed again, mostly, as things closed on him. She looked at it as a carter looks at a wheel. She washed it from her skin and bound it fresh in her cloth, tight, and did not put her hands on it and go still.

"I've a finding on you," she said, to the calf. "I'll not run it again on a hillside. At the ford, when I've a table and I'm not counting days." She tied off the cloth. "You'll keep till then. You've been keeping for years."

---

The route came out on the second night, at the fire, because Karis asked for it, for the map.

Brom had lit it, with a flint. Karis sat by it with the book on her knee and the pen in the scarred hand; she had been looking at the healer for two days with the look she gave a primary source, and had held the question, which Cael marked, because Karis holding a question was Karis paying for something. What she paid for was the map. "Where does it go," she said. "The route. For the map. I've Oxhollow and Thornwater and nothing between."

Oryn named seven.

She named them in order, as a carter names the fords on a road, and Karis wrote them as she said them, and two of the seven Cael knew. Oxhollow, where they were going. Thornwater, where he had lain in a grain loft with a calf that said eleven. The five between he had never heard of, and he did not ask, and the healer did not describe them; they were names, and then they were ink, and Karis drew a line through the seven that came back round to the first.

"Twenty days," Oryn said. "Round. Two at a hold, near enough, and the road between, and the road's the part that doesn't move. A hold can wait a day. The road can't be walked faster than it's walked." She had the mule's lead in her hand and was working a burr out of it. "I've walked it round thirty-odd times. I've been late to a hold eleven of them. This is twelve."

"What do they owe you?" Karis. The research voice; the question meant exactly.

"Oxhollow shoes the mule and keeps a table I don't have to carry. Thornwater feeds me. One of the five gave me this coat two winters back and won't hear about it." She did not look up from the burr. "And I owe them. There's a hand at Oxhollow I said I'd look at again and haven't. There's a child three stops on that had a fever the day I left, and I don't know. That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."

"You keep that in a book."

"No."

