## E. Two calendars in ch22

- **Reset (ch22:235):** Teague's window now opens "It was still the eighty-fourth day, and the light was going off the hill above Oxhollow, when Teague counted his three."
- **"Three weeks" (ch22:291):** "There was a healer's tape on him. That meant ribs; and ribs, by her rule, were three weeks under linen, counted from whichever day the mountain had done it."
- **Oryn's sum (ch22:207–229), as it now stands on the page:** broken on the 78th; twenty-one days by the rule. Her hands say that he will be sound some days sooner, but she cannot read him, so she cannot know it, and she will not lift a tape on a guess: **whole on the 99th**. The long reading wants him whole and still and wants most of a day of her: **the 100th is the first morning she can run it.** She is at Thornwater on the 93rd (he is fifteen days from the break); her stop ends on the 95th. **"A week after she had gone" is now "four days after she had gone"**, and the round would not bring her back for twenty days. From the 95th to the 100th is five days. Four of them she can spend at Thornwater's own table as honest work, since the grain is moving and its bench will fill again; they make her late at six gates, and she knows whom she keeps waiting. The fifth, the day of the reading, mends nobody on her route and is owed by no hold: "If it was to be had, it must be added." **She put a day on the route.** The paragraphs on wanting are unchanged and now stand between the sum and the decision.
- This agrees with the day table (Thornwater 93; tape off 99; the reading on the 100th) and with the later lateness count (nine days late at Thornwater on the 104th means a stop that should have ended on the 95th). **For the coordinator:** Movement 4 has her walk the Fallow road with the crew on the 94th–98th instead of sitting at Thornwater's table. Her plan here is what she intends on the 92nd; she changes it at the gate on the 93rd ("You're a stop. I've added you."). If the ledger would sooner have her plan nothing for the four days, the paragraph beginning "She took it out of the round" can lose its second half without touching the last line.

